  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 121 AM CST Mon Nov 9 2026\n\nCURRENT OVERNIGHT - TODAY:\n\nSurface high pressure currently situated over the Northland is\nleading to light winds. Meanwhile, a low pressure system is\nmoving into Manitoba, with attendant warm and cold fronts\nextending southward into North Dakota. Mid to high-level cloud\ncover is increasing across the Northland ahead of this system.\nAs today progresses, the low pressure will track east through\nnorthwest Ontario, bringing the fronts through the Northland,\nwith the cold front exiting east of the area this evening. CAMs\nvary regarding whether or not very light rain or sprinkles can \nreach the ground (10-20% chance) in far northern Minnesota into\nthe Arrowhead this morning through midday, but a wedge of dry \nair in the 700 mb to surface layer could keep this precipitation\nfrom reaching the ground. Very little to no measurable rainfall\nis expected.\n\nExpect clouds to clear out from west to east this afternoon,\nwith seasonably warm high temperatures in the upper 40s to upper\n50s this afternoon. South to southwest winds in the morning will\nturn to westerly this afternoon and tonight behind the cold\nfront. Some katabatic winds are forecast for the North Shore\nthis evening and tonight with wind gusts of 20 to 30 mph.\n\nTUESDAY:\n\nAnother seasonably mild and dry day is in store for Tuesday as\nhigh pressure slides through the Upper Mississippi Valley, with\nhigh temperatures in the mid/upper 40s north to low/mid 50s\nsouth. Expect partly cloudy skies during the day, with \nincreasing cloud cover in the evening as some mid- and upper-level\nshortwave troughing energy approaches from the west. \n\nTUESDAY EVENING - FRIDAY:\n\nThe next chance for light precipitation arrives Tuesday evening\ninto Wednesday as the aforementioned shortwave trough and an\nattendant surface low move through the region. The best synoptic\nforcing with the shortwave Tuesday night into early Wednesday will\nbe across southern portions of the Northland, but a lobe of\nadditional vorticity moving south out of northwest Ontario\ndaytime Wednesday will also bring low-end light precipitation \npotential (10-30% chances). Little to no measurable rainfall is \nexpected at the surface. Some light flurries/snowflakes could \nmix in for the Iron Range and Arrowhead daytime Wednesday, but \nwe do not anticipate any snow accumulation. \n\nFor Wednesday PM into early Friday morning, much colder 850 mb\ntemperatures of -5 to -10 degC will move overhead, promoting\nlight lake-effect precipitation for the South Shore due to \nrelatively warm Lake Superior water surface of 6 to 9 degC \nabove zero. Moisture availability has trended slightly higher\nrelative to previous forecasts, so we have bumped up forecast\nQPF slightly, though it still remains limited. This \nprecipitation will be largely rain daytime Wednesday, \nbut transitions to a rain/snow mix Wednesday evening and mostly\nsnow Wednesday night through Thursday before ending Thursday\nnight/early Friday morning as high pressure settles back into \nthe area starting on Friday. Snowfall amount probabilities have\ntrended upwards, with a 70-90% chance for 1\" or greater in the\nAshland and Iron County snowbelts, but potential for 2\" or\ngreater currently sits at only 10-30%.\n\nExpect near to slightly below normal temperatures middle to \nlater portion of the work week with high temperatures in the \nupper 30s to low 40s.\n\nNEXT WEEKEND:\n\nHigh pressure lingers over the Upper Midwest for next weekend,\nwith southerly winds and mid-level ridging pushing high\ntemperatures back into the upper 30s to mid 40s on Saturday and\n40s to low 50s on Sunday.",
